fdc5b6f9a80aa4ec","67ffa4d5b4bac836","0a758c6afad1ddbd","6ce313850f0594e0","19ee2920a7c67943","d0f4eb4c7c675ffc"],"findings":"**Verdict on the annotations:** mostly accurate. The decision round-trip, reader validation, old-reader compatibility and adopt/carry copying all check out. I found one real defect (the 16 KB cap silently drops decisions the CLI accepted) and a few places where the docs or grading overstate the code.\n\nTest run: `node --test test/decisions.test.ts` passes 9/9 (the author's evidence holds). I did not run the full suite or typecheck.\n\n## 1. Mismatches\n\n- **decision-records / decide-cli: the 16 KB cap silently drops decisions the CLI accepts. Verified.** The CLI checks `finding` and `note` at 2000 characters each, but `JSON.stringify` writes control characters as 6-byte `\\u00XX` escapes. Two 2000-char control-character fields make a line of about 24 KB. The reader drops any decision line over 16 KB. In a scratch repo, `--decide … --finding <2000×\\x01> --note <2000×\\x01>` returned exit 0 and printed \"recorded\". `reviews` then showed \"decisions on this review: none yet\". The docs say a decision line \"is a few hundred bytes; anything this long is junk\", which is not what the write path produces. Fix: check `JSON.stringify(record).length <= MAX_DECISION_LINE` in `checkDecision`/before write, so the write fails loudly. **verified**\n- **decide-cli / README \"Decisions\": decisions after `--complete` are accepted.** The docs say `complete` means \"no more decisions are coming\", but nothing enforces it. In the scratch repo a `late` decision after `--complete` was recorded and counted (\"1 rejected · complete\"). Either reject writes after complete, or say the marker is advisory. **verified**\n- **decision-display: a clean review with a stray decision does print a decisions block in `reviews`.** The code's condition is `r.verdict !== 'clean' || ds.length`, which prints it. `show` ignores clean reviews entirely, and the docs say \"A review whose verdict is clean needs none\". The two views disagree. **read** (the test only checks `show`)\n- **decide-cli: `--by` fallback is undocumented.** When there is no `--by` and no `git config user.name`, the code writes `by: \"unknown\"`. The README says `by` is `git config user.name` or `--by`. **read**\n- **decide-cli: the confirmation line echoes the finding unfiltered.** `console.log` prints `record.finding` raw. This is the arbiter's own input, so the risk is low, but it is outside the \"plain() on everything shown\" claim. **read**\n- **decision-records: the bidi list is incomplete.** `plain()` covers `\\u202a-\\u202e` and `\\u2066-\\u2069` but not `\\u200e`, `\\u200f` or `\\u061c`. Low risk. **read**\n\n## 2. Misgraded necessity\n\n- **sanitise-output (labelled supporting, serves decision-display) is really a pre-existing hardening.** Review findings and commit subjects in `reviews` and `show` were already printed before this change. The hostile-review test covers old record fields (agent, stage, model), not decisions. Only the `readDecisions` path is new. This group is better labelled `defensive`, or split into its own commit. It also changes existing behaviour: `adopt`/`carry` now write cleaned review records. The author does flag that. **read**\n- **decision-tests (supporting)** is fine. **decision-docs (supporting)** is fine.\n- The `decision-records`, `decide-cli`, `decision-display` and `decision-follow` groups are all genuinely required for the task.\n\n## 3. Removal candidates\n\n- None needed for the task. The `sanitise-output` group could be dropped from this change without breaking decisions, but then `decision-display` would print raw text. Splitting it out is the cleaner option.\n\n## 4. Simplifications\n\n- `readDecisions` calls `readReviews(sha)`, which re-parses the whole note. `reviews` and `reviewsLine` then read the note again per commit (`readReviews` + `readDecisions` + `reviewStatus`). Efficiency is minor for typical notes, but one parse per commit would be simpler. **read**\n- The `ok` expression in `readDecisions` is hard to scan, especially the `complete` branch inside `&&`. Splitting it into a `validDecision(r)` helper would make it easier to review. **read**\n\n## 5. Missing risks\n\n- **Duplicate decisions inflate counts.** Re-running the arbiter adds the same finding again and it counts twice. The author lists this, but there is no dedupe or warning. **read**\n- **Silent drops.** Besides the 16 KB case, the reader drops any decision whose field fails validation, with no feedback to the writer. The writer should check the same rules the reader enforces. **read**\n- **Cap is on the line, not the field.** The docs say review lines are uncapped and point to the TODO. A reviewer could still send a huge review line from a remote. The author lists this. **read**\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
