 cannot drift between the two. Size: small. Checked: **read**.\n\n3. **`plain()` uses two almost identical regexes.** Where: `src/reviews.ts:180-187`. The only difference is whether `\\n` and `\\t` are kept. Suggestion: one character class plus a replacer, for example `.replace(/[\\x00-\\x1f\\x7f-\\x9f\\u2028\\u2029\\u202a-\\u202e\\u2066-\\u2069]/g, (c) => multiline && (c === '\\n' || c === '\\t') ? c : ' ')`. Benefit: the long regex is written once, so the two cannot diverge. Size: trivial. Checked: **read**. Equivalence follows from the character sets, but I did not run it.\n\n4. **Nothing in the repo to reuse for sanitising.** A search of `src` found no other ANSI or control-character stripping; the only copy is the one in `plain()`. No change suggested there. A repeated-logic check of `decisionSummary`/`reviewsLine` found two different `complete` rules (per review in `reviews`, all open reviews in `show`). They are intentional, so I would leave them.\n\n5. **Minor.** `checkDecision` (`src/reviews.ts`, the `for ... of [['finding', ...], ['note', ...]]` loop) could be two plain `if` statements. Probably not worth changing.\n\nOverall the change is readable and mostly lean. The main cost is the repeated note reads (item 1), and items 2 and 3 are small cleanups. I did not apply or test any of these in the clone, so every item is **read**, not **verified**.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
